7
00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:20.545
Ch 05, Ch 13, Ch 14, Ch 26, What you will build:

8
00:00:20.825 --> 00:00:31.513
How the grocery platform changes its database schemas, its message formats and its APIs without downtime and without a flag-day where everything must upgrade at once.

9
00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:41.756
Every lab runs against real PostgreSQL 16 or real protoc 28.3 and measures the blocking, the compatibility and the bloat directly.

10
00:00:43.106 --> 00:00:45.005
L1: which DDL blocks.

11
00:00:45.285 --> 00:01:06.831
On a 2,000,000-row table under live traffic, a volatile-default ADD COLUMN, a NOT NULL validation, and a plain CREATE INDEX each stalled live queries for the full duration of a table-scan (up to the 8 s locktimeout); a constant-default add was metadata-only (14 ms) and CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY never blocked writes.

12
00:01:07.111 --> 00:01:09.095
L2: expand or contract.

13
00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:26.229
A column rename + retype (totalpaise bigint, then amountrupees numeric) ran through six phases, expand, dual-write, batched backfill, validate, read-new, contract, with a writer and a reader hitting the table the whole time and zero errors, zero mismatches.

14
00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.011
L3: message schema compatibility.

15
00:01:30.291 --> 00:01:40.061
Real protoc encode or decode showed adding fields is forward- and backward-compatible, while reusing a field number with a new type causes silent data loss.

16
00:01:40.341 --> 00:01:49.045
A descriptor-based checker replicated a schema registry's BACKWARD or FORWARD or FULL verdicts: it accepted v2, rejected v3.

17
00:01:49.325 --> 00:01:51.395
L4: large-table backfill.

18
00:01:51.675 --> 00:02:06.331
Backfilling 3,000,000 rows with one big UPDATE blocked a concurrent writer until the 8 s locktimeout fired; the batched form (25k rows or commit) capped that wait at ~100 ms for the same end result.

19
00:02:06.611 --> 00:02:07.592
Reading time:

20
00:02:07.872 --> 00:02:09.792
120 min + code.

21
00:02:11.142 --> 00:02:11.995
Part 1.

22
00:02:12.275 --> 00:02:14.195
Introduction and Why It Matters.

23
00:02:15.445 --> 00:02:18.176
A story: the migration that took the store down.

24
00:02:18.456 --> 00:02:26.221
The team needed to change orders.total from an integer number of paise to a decimal number of rupees, and rename it to amount.

25
00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:32.411
A junior engineer wrote the obvious migration and ran it during a Tuesday afternoon deploy:

26
00:02:33.761 --> 00:02:35.361
Here is the SQL code.

27
00:02:38.211 --> 00:02:39.853
Three things went wrong at once:

28
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:47.003
The ALTER TYPE rewrote the whole table, 40 million rows, holding an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock the entire time.

29
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:50.035
Every read and write on orders queued behind it.

30
00:02:50.315 --> 00:02:51.573
Checkout timed out.

31
00:02:51.853 --> 00:02:54.371
The store was effectively down for nine minutes.

32
00:02:55.721 --> 00:02:59.369
The rename broke every running instance of the old code instantly.

33
00:02:59.649 --> 00:03:09.761
The deploy was a rolling one, so for the first two minutes half the fleet was still running the version that did SELECT total FROM orders, which now errored with column does not exist.

34
00:03:10.041 --> 00:03:14.350
Even the pods that hadn't been updated yet started throwing 500s.

35
00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.465
The order-events topic broke too.

36
00:03:16.745 --> 00:03:28.521
The OrderPlaced event carried totalpaise; a downstream analytics consumer that hadn't been redeployed suddenly saw a renamed field and started dropping every message to its dead-letter queue.

37
00:03:29.871 --> 00:03:37.401
The rollback was worse than the deploy: reversing the ALTER TYPE meant another full-table rewrite and another nine-minute lock.

38
00:03:37.681 --> 00:03:40.647
The postmortem's conclusion was not be more careful.

39
00:03:40.927 --> 00:03:43.295
It was that the shape of the migration was wrong.

40
00:03:43.575 --> 00:03:55.116
A schema change on a live system is not one atomic step, it is a sequence of individually safe, backward-compatible steps that let old and new code coexist while the fleet rolls.

41
00:03:55.396 --> 00:04:00.089
You never rename in place; you add, dual-write, backfill, switch reads, then drop.

42
00:04:00.369 --> 00:04:05.084
You never rewrite a 40-million-row table under a lock when you can do it in batches.

43
00:04:05.364 --> 00:04:14.772
And a field on a message bus is a contract with consumers you don't control the deploy timing of, so you evolve it under compatibility rules, not by editing it.

44
00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:18.426
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

