86
00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:21.922
Part 2.

87
00:06:22.202 --> 00:06:24.079
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

88
00:06:25.329 --> 00:06:26.247
Intuition.

89
00:06:26.527 --> 00:06:30.409
Two facts make schema change hard, and everything else follows from them:

90
00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:31.905
The data is live.

91
00:06:32.185 --> 00:06:36.025
The table has millions of rows and is being read and written right now.

92
00:06:36.305 --> 00:06:40.743
A change that locks it, or rewrites it, is an outage for as long as it runs.

93
00:06:41.023 --> 00:06:42.217
The code is rolling.

94
00:06:42.497 --> 00:06:50.796
During a deploy, and during a rollback, old code and new code run at the same time, against the same schema and the same messages.

95
00:06:51.076 --> 00:06:54.468
Any change that only one of them understands breaks the other.

96
00:06:55.818 --> 00:07:02.901
So the golden rule is: every intermediate state must be compatible with both the old and the new version of the code.

97
00:07:03.181 --> 00:07:11.714
You get there by making changes additive and reversible first, and destructive only at the very end, once nothing depends on the old shape.

98
00:07:11.994 --> 00:07:19.717
This single idea, expand, then contract, reappears for columns, for messages, for APIs, and for whole datastores.

99
00:07:21.067 --> 00:07:22.965
The two compatibility directions.

100
00:07:23.245 --> 00:07:28.195
Every evolution question reduces to two directions, and it is worth being precise:

101
00:07:28.475 --> 00:07:33.957
Backward compatible: new code or a new reader can process data written by the old schema.

102
00:07:34.237 --> 00:07:35.923
(You can deploy the reader first.).

103
00:07:36.203 --> 00:07:41.664
Forward compatible: old code or an old reader can process data written by the new schema.

104
00:07:41.944 --> 00:07:43.672
(You can deploy the writer first.).

105
00:07:43.952 --> 00:07:45.616
Full compatible: both.

106
00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:52.235
Why both matter: in a rolling deploy you cannot control which pod handles which request.

107
00:07:52.515 --> 00:08:00.281
If a new-code pod writes a row (or a message) and an old-code pod reads it a millisecond later, you need forward compatibility.

108
00:08:00.561 --> 00:08:05.851
If an old-code pod wrote it and a new-code pod reads it, you need backward compatibility.

109
00:08:06.131 --> 00:08:15.987
During the overlap window you need both, that is, full compatibility, which is exactly what additive changes give you and what renames or retypes destroy.

110
00:08:16.267 --> 00:08:18.721
L3 measures this on real protobuf.

111
00:08:20.071 --> 00:08:21.671
What actually locks a table.

112
00:08:21.951 --> 00:08:23.572
Not all DDL is equal.

113
00:08:23.852 --> 00:08:28.353
On PostgreSQL (other engines differ in detail, same principles):

114
00:08:29.703 --> 00:08:31.047
Here is a quick table:

115
00:08:31.327 --> 00:08:33.247
Operation, and Lock, and Cost.

116
00:08:33.527 --> 00:08:35.895
ADD COLUMN nullable or constant default.

117
00:08:36.175 --> 00:08:36.879
Lock:

118
00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:39.591
ACCESS EXCLUSIVE but metadata-only.

119
00:08:39.871 --> 00:08:42.815
Cost: microseconds (PG 11 plus).

120
00:08:43.095 --> 00:08:45.101
ADD COLUMN with a volatile default.

121
00:08:45.381 --> 00:08:46.085
Lock:

122
00:08:46.365 --> 00:08:48.989
ACCESS EXCLUSIVE + full table rewrite.

123
00:08:49.269 --> 00:08:51.701
Cost: scans + rewrites every row.

124
00:08:51.981 --> 00:08:53.666
ALTER COLUMN TYPE (most).

125
00:08:53.946 --> 00:08:54.650
Lock:

126
00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:56.935
ACCESS EXCLUSIVE + rewrite.

127
00:08:57.215 --> 00:08:59.647
Cost: scans + rewrites every row.

128
00:08:59.927 --> 00:09:01.335
SET NOT NULL (naive).

129
00:09:01.615 --> 00:09:02.319
Lock:

130
00:09:02.599 --> 00:09:05.437
ACCESS EXCLUSIVE + full scan to validate.

131
00:09:05.717 --> 00:09:07.445
Cost: scans every row.

132
00:09:07.725 --> 00:09:08.877
ADD CONSTRAINT...

133
00:09:09.157 --> 00:09:09.861
CHECK.

134
00:09:10.141 --> 00:09:10.845
Lock:

135
00:09:11.125 --> 00:09:13.002
ACCESS EXCLUSIVE + scan.

136
00:09:13.282 --> 00:09:15.010
Cost: scans every row.

137
00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:17.512
ADD CONSTRAINT...

138
00:09:17.792 --> 00:09:18.496
CHECK...

139
00:09:18.776 --> 00:09:19.651
NOT VALID.

140
00:09:19.931 --> 00:09:22.725
Lock: brief ACCESS EXCLUSIVE (metadata).

141
00:09:23.005 --> 00:09:24.136
Cost: no scan.

142
00:09:24.416 --> 00:09:25.760
VALIDATE CONSTRAINT.

143
00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:26.744
Lock:

144
00:09:27.024 --> 00:09:28.453
SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE.

145
00:09:28.733 --> 00:09:31.272
Cost: scans, but does not block writes.

146
00:09:31.552 --> 00:09:32.683
CREATE INDEX.

147
00:09:32.963 --> 00:09:33.667
Lock:

148
00:09:33.947 --> 00:09:35.184
SHARE (blocks writes).

149
00:09:35.464 --> 00:09:37.768
Cost: builds the index while writes wait.

150
00:09:38.048 --> 00:09:39.605
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY.

151
00:09:39.885 --> 00:09:40.589
Lock:

152
00:09:40.869 --> 00:09:42.299
SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE.

153
00:09:42.579 --> 00:09:45.096
Cost: slower build, does not block writes.

154
00:09:46.446 --> 00:09:58.115
The pattern: the dangerous operations take a strong lock (ACCESS EXCLUSIVE or SHARE) and hold it for the duration of a full-table operation, so live queries stall for seconds.

155
00:09:58.395 --> 00:10:06.630
The safe forms either touch only catalog metadata (instant) or take a weaker lock that lets writes through (CONCURRENTLY, VALIDATE).

156
00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:09.193
L1 measures each of these directly.

157
00:10:10.543 --> 00:10:11.289
Warning:

158
00:10:11.569 --> 00:10:13.937
Always set a lock timeout for migrations.

159
00:10:14.217 --> 00:10:19.465
Run migrations with SET locktimeout = '3s' (and often statementtimeout).

160
00:10:19.745 --> 00:10:36.492
If the migration can't get its lock quickly, because a long transaction is holding it, you want it to fail fast and retry, not to queue an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock that then blocks every new query behind it (a lock queue pileup that turns a fast DDL into an outage).

161
00:10:36.772 --> 00:10:44.260
L1 runs with locktimeout = 8s, which is why its stalled queries error out at 8 s instead of hanging forever.

162
00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.255
Expand or contract (the parallel change).

163
00:10:48.535 --> 00:10:54.466
The canonical safe way to change a column's name, type, or representation, in six phases:

164
00:10:54.746 --> 00:11:00.399
Expand, add the new column (nullable, no volatile default, then metadata-only, no lock).

165
00:11:00.679 --> 00:11:04.626
Dual-write, deploy code that writes both the old and the new column.

166
00:11:04.906 --> 00:11:09.898
Old code still writes only the old one; both are always consistent for new rows.

167
00:11:10.178 --> 00:11:18.690
Backfill, copy the old value into the new column for historical rows, in batches (L4), skipping rows the dual-write already filled.

168
00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:28.317
Validate, now every row has a value; add the NOT NULL or CHECK invariant using NOT VALID + VALIDATE so you don't block writes.

169
00:11:28.597 --> 00:11:31.456
Read-new, deploy code that reads the new column.

170
00:11:31.736 --> 00:11:35.427
Keep dual-writing so a rollback to the previous version still works.

171
00:11:35.707 --> 00:11:45.008
Contract, once no deployed code reads or writes the old column, drop it (the only destructive step, done last, easily its own migration).

172
00:11:45.288 --> 00:11:53.843
Each phase is independently deployable and each is backward+forward compatible with the one before it, so a rollback at any point is safe.

173
00:11:54.123 --> 00:11:58.603
L2 runs all six against real PostgreSQL with live traffic.

174
00:11:59.953 --> 00:12:01.318
Backfills without tears.

175
00:12:01.598 --> 00:12:04.350
A backfill sets a value on many existing rows.

176
00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:14.529
The naive UPDATE t SET c =... with no bound touches every row in one transaction, which: holds row locks on everything it has touched until commit,

177
00:12:14.809 --> 00:12:32.729
concurrent writers to those rows block;. writes one enormous WAL record and creates one huge batch of dead tuples that autovacuum cannot reclaim until the transaction ends (table + index bloat);. if it fails at 90%, the whole thing rolls back and you start over.

178
00:12:34.079 --> 00:12:41.481
The production form is batched: loop over the primary-key range in chunks (for example, 25k rows), UPDATE...

179
00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:45.772
WHERE id BETWEEN lo AND hi AND not yet done, and commit each batch.

180
00:12:46.052 --> 00:12:53.668
Locks last milliseconds, writers slip between batches, autovacuum keeps up, and a failure only loses the current batch.

181
00:12:53.948 --> 00:13:03.036
Drive batches off an indexed key range, never LIMIT or OFFSET (which re-scans from the start each time, turning O(n) into O(n²)).

182
00:13:03.316 --> 00:13:10.548
L4 measures the difference: same total time, but 8,000 ms of blocking versus ~100 ms.

183
00:13:11.898 --> 00:13:13.989
Message and event schema versioning.

184
00:13:14.269 --> 00:13:16.637
A row in your own database you can migrate.

185
00:13:16.917 --> 00:13:28.479
A message on a bus (Ch 14) is a contract with consumers you don't own and can't redeploy on your schedule, and with events already persisted in the log that you will re-read months later.

186
00:13:28.759 --> 00:13:40.471
So message schemas evolve under strict rules, enforced by a schema registry (Confluent Schema Registry, Apicurio, Buf) that rejects an incompatible schema at publish time.

187
00:13:40.751 --> 00:13:43.354
Serialization formats and how they evolve:

188
00:13:44.704 --> 00:13:53.856
Protobuf, fields keyed by number; unknown fields are skipped by old readers (forward-compatible) and default in new readers (backward-compatible).

189
00:13:54.136 --> 00:13:57.272
Safe: add fields, rename (number is the identity).

190
00:13:57.552 --> 00:14:01.499
Unsafe: reuse a number, change a type, change a field's semantics.

191
00:14:01.779 --> 00:14:04.915
Reserve retired numbers so nobody reuses them.

192
00:14:05.195 --> 00:14:11.000
Avro, reader and writer schemas are both used; fields need defaults to be removable or addable compatibly.

193
00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:16.248
The registry stores writer schemas and resolves against the reader's.

194
00:14:17.598 --> 00:14:28.115
Thrift or JSON Schema, similar additive rules; JSON is self-describing but has no enforced compatibility unless you add a registry or validator.

195
00:14:28.395 --> 00:14:32.278
Compatibility modes a registry enforces (Confluent's names):

196
00:14:33.628 --> 00:14:34.972
Here is a quick table:

197
00:14:35.252 --> 00:14:37.620
Mode, and Guarantee, and Deploy order.

198
00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:38.732
BACKWARD.

199
00:14:39.012 --> 00:14:41.423
Guarantee: new schema reads old data.

200
00:14:41.703 --> 00:14:43.751
Deploy order: consumers first.

201
00:14:44.031 --> 00:14:44.863
FORWARD.

202
00:14:45.143 --> 00:14:47.617
Guarantee: old schema reads new data.

203
00:14:47.897 --> 00:14:49.839
Deploy order: producers first.

204
00:14:50.119 --> 00:14:50.801
FULL.

205
00:14:51.081 --> 00:14:52.383
Guarantee: both.

206
00:14:52.663 --> 00:14:54.135
Deploy order: either.

207
00:14:54.415 --> 00:14:55.097
NONE.

208
00:14:55.377 --> 00:14:56.913
Guarantee: no checks.

209
00:14:57.193 --> 00:14:59.071
Deploy order: you're on your own.

210
00:15:00.421 --> 00:15:05.583
L3 builds a real descriptor-based checker that produces exactly these verdicts.

211
00:15:06.933 --> 00:15:09.429
Blue-green and versioned schema migrations.

212
00:15:09.709 --> 00:15:11.907
Versioned, forward-only migrations.

213
00:15:12.187 --> 00:15:22.533
Tools like Flyway and Liquibase apply numbered migration files once each, tracked in a schemahistory table, as part of the deploy pipeline (Ch 26).

214
00:15:22.813 --> 00:15:34.184
Migrations are forward-only: you don't write down scripts for production (a rollback is a new forward migration), because down-migrations are rarely tested and often destructive.

215
00:15:34.464 --> 00:15:35.979
Migrate before deploy.

216
00:15:36.259 --> 00:15:45.432
Run the (backward-compatible) migration as a separate pipeline step before the new code rolls out, so the schema is ready when the first new pod starts.

217
00:15:45.712 --> 00:15:50.939
Because expand-phase migrations are additive, the still-running old code is unaffected.

218
00:15:52.289 --> 00:15:53.633
Blue-green + schema.

219
00:15:53.913 --> 00:16:07.502
Blue-green (Ch 26) swaps whole environments, but blue and green usually share one database, so the schema must be compatible with both colors at once, which is exactly expand or contract again.

220
00:16:07.782 --> 00:16:13.329
A separate database per color needs replication between them and is rare for stateful data.

221
00:16:14.679 --> 00:16:15.980
API versioning.

222
00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:25.391
The same compatibility thinking applies at the HTTP edge, where clients (mobile apps, partners) upgrade on their own schedule, sometimes never:

223
00:16:25.671 --> 00:16:33.564
Additive, in place: adding an optional request field or a new response field is backward-compatible; do it without a new version.

224
00:16:33.844 --> 00:16:38.025
Clients ignore unknown response fields (be lenient in what you accept).

225
00:16:38.305 --> 00:16:45.111
New version only for true breaks: removing or renaming a field, changing a type, or changing semantics.

226
00:16:45.391 --> 00:16:50.319
Expose or v2, run it alongside or v1, and migrate clients over.

227
00:16:51.669 --> 00:16:52.842
Versioning styles:

228
00:16:53.122 --> 00:17:07.714
URI (/v2 or orders), visible, cache-friendly, most common; header or media-type (Accept: application or vnd.shop.v2 plusjson), cleaner URLs, harder to test; query param, discouraged.

229
00:17:07.994 --> 00:17:17.594
Sunset gracefully: announce, emit a Deprecation and Sunset header on or v1 responses, monitor who's still calling it, and only then retire.

230
00:17:17.874 --> 00:17:19.389
See Ch 04.

231
00:17:20.739 --> 00:17:22.701
Data migration between stores.

232
00:17:22.981 --> 00:17:39.387
Moving a dataset from one store to another (Postgres, then a new sharded cluster, or a table, then a search index, or a monolith DB, then a service's own DB during a strangler migration, Ch 16) is expand or contract at the system scale:

233
00:17:39.667 --> 00:17:48.989
Dual-write to old and new stores (via the app, or via CDC or outbox so you don't rely on a distributed transaction, Ch 13).

234
00:17:49.269 --> 00:17:51.616
Backfill history into the new store (batched).

235
00:17:51.896 --> 00:18:02.477
Reconcile, continuously compare old versus new (row counts, checksums, sampled diffs) until they match; fix the dual-write bugs the reconciliation finds.

236
00:18:03.827 --> 00:18:09.609
Read-switch, move reads to the new store behind a flag, canary it, keep the old store as a fallback.

237
00:18:09.889 --> 00:18:13.622
Decommission the old store once reads have been stable on the new one.

238
00:18:13.902 --> 00:18:20.643
The reconciliation step is the one teams skip and regret: dual-write bugs are silent until you compare.

239
00:18:21.993 --> 00:18:23.999
Backward-compatible by construction.

240
00:18:24.279 --> 00:18:29.164
The most senior habit is to design changes so the unsafe step never exists:

241
00:18:29.444 --> 00:18:32.601
Never rename, add the new name, migrate, drop the old.

242
00:18:32.881 --> 00:18:36.231
(In-place rename has no compatible intermediate state.).

243
00:18:36.511 --> 00:18:41.609
Never change a type in place, add a new column of the new type (expand or contract).

244
00:18:41.889 --> 00:18:46.561
Never reuse a message field number or enum value, reserve retired ones.

245
00:18:46.841 --> 00:18:52.537
Make columns nullable or give them constant defaults so ADD COLUMN stays metadata-only.

246
00:18:53.887 --> 00:18:57.237
Add invariants in two steps (NOT VALID then VALIDATE).

247
00:18:57.517 --> 00:19:04.514
Treat every externally-visible shape (table used by other services, message, API) as append-only.

