The checker flags the type change and the moved field and returns INCOMPATIBLE, the registry rejects the producer at publish time, which is how you avoid a flag-day break on the bus.

427
00:36:25.699 --> 00:36:34.125
Protobuf keys on number, not name, so a pure rename (same number) is wire-safe; it's number reuse and type changes that corrupt.

428
00:36:34.405 --> 00:36:37.541
Reserve retired numbers so nobody reuses them.

429
00:36:38.891 --> 00:36:39.638
Note:

430
00:36:39.918 --> 00:36:56.835
Production shape (VERIFY: needs a running registry or Kafka). // Confluent Schema Registry set to FULL: the producer's serializer registers the schema and the registry. // rejects an incompatible one before the first message is sent.

431
00:36:57.115 --> 00:37:17.681
Enforce the same check in CI on every schema PR. props.put(schema.registry.url, http://registry:8081);. props.put(auto.register.schemas, true); // registry runs the compatibility check on register. props.put(use.latest.version, true);.

432
00:37:19.031 --> 00:37:24.471
// CLI or CI gate: mvn io.confluenttest-compatibility.

433
00:37:25.821 --> 00:37:29.959
L4, Backfilling a huge table: one big UPDATE versus batched.

434
00:37:30.239 --> 00:37:31.178
Requirement:

435
00:37:31.458 --> 00:37:38.221
Backfill a column across 3,000,000 rows while the table is live, without blocking writers or bloating the table.

436
00:37:38.501 --> 00:37:51.045
We backfill amountrupees on a 3M-row table two ways, one unbounded UPDATE, then 25k-row committed batches, while a live writer updates random rows and records its worst wait.

437
00:37:52.395 --> 00:37:55.381
Here is the Java code, BackfillL4.java.

438
00:37:55.661 --> 00:37:59.928
It runs to 133 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

439
00:38:00.208 --> 00:38:11.237
BackfillL4.java, run with: java -Duser.timezone=UTC -cp or tmp or pgjdbc.jar BackfillL4.java.

440
00:38:11.517 --> 00:38:17.171
The recurring backfill a column across a huge table problem on REAL PostgreSQL.

441
00:38:17.451 --> 00:38:24.576
We compare two ways to set. amountrupees on 3,000,000 rows while a live writer keeps updating random rows:

442
00:38:27.426 --> 00:38:28.343
On screen:

443
00:38:28.623 --> 00:38:29.818
3,000,000 rows.

444
00:38:30.098 --> 00:38:33.127
A live writer updates random rows the whole time.

445
00:38:33.407 --> 00:38:35.647
We backfill. amountrupees two ways.

446
00:38:35.927 --> 00:38:37.229
A) ONE big UPDATE:

447
00:38:37.509 --> 00:38:45.679
11,613 ms total concurrent writer blocked up to 8,011 ms dead. tuples after:

448
00:38:45.959 --> 00:38:47.325
3,000,085.

449
00:38:47.605 --> 00:38:50.165
B) BATCHED (25k or txn):

450
00:38:50.445 --> 00:38:57.826
11,108 ms total concurrent writer blocked up to 100 ms dead. tuples after:

451
00:38:58.106 --> 00:39:15.087
3,002,345 (120 batches). then the single UPDATE locks every row it rewrites for the entire statement, so a. concurrent writer touching one of. those rows waits behind the whole backfill -- here it blocked until locktimeout.

452
00:39:15.367 --> 00:39:16.434
(8 s) fired.

453
00:39:16.714 --> 00:39:23.733
Both forms create. ~3M dead tuples, but the single UPDATE holds them in ONE uncommitted transaction.

454
00:39:24.013 --> 00:39:25.527
The rest is in the chapter.

455
00:39:28.377 --> 00:39:29.231
Reading it:

456
00:39:29.511 --> 00:39:40.113
The single UPDATE locked every row it rewrote for the whole statement, so the concurrent writer, when it hit a row inside the backfill, waited until the 8 s locktimeout fired.

457
00:39:40.393 --> 00:39:46.687
The batched form capped that wait at ~100 ms for the same end result and same total time.

458
00:39:46.967 --> 00:40:04.225
Both create ~3M dead tuples, but the single UPDATE holds them in one uncommitted transaction that autovacuum cannot touch until it ends; the batched form commits every 25k rows, so autovacuum can reclaim as it goes and each lock is momentary.

459
00:40:05.575 --> 00:40:22.599
Production hardening (§2.5, Q18): drive batches off an indexed key range (not OFFSET), make each batch idempotent (WHERE amountrupees IS NULL) so a crash resumes cleanly, persist a checkpoint cursor, and throttle on replication lag.

460
00:40:22.879 --> 00:40:26.378
This is the only backfill shape that's safe on a live table.

461
00:40:27.728 --> 00:40:28.667
Part 6.

462
00:40:28.947 --> 00:40:30.227
Production Concerns.

463
00:40:31.477 --> 00:40:32.629
Failure modes.

464
00:40:32.909 --> 00:40:34.253
Here is a quick table:

465
00:40:34.533 --> 00:40:36.730
Failure, and Cause, and Mitigation.

466
00:40:37.010 --> 00:40:38.311
Migration outage.

467
00:40:38.591 --> 00:40:39.359
Cause:

468
00:40:39.639 --> 00:40:44.930
In-place ALTER TYPE or volatile-default add or plain CREATE INDEX on a big table.

469
00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:46.213
Mitigation:

470
00:40:46.493 --> 00:40:53.127
Online-DDL rules (§2.3, L1); locktimeout so it fails fast instead of pileup.

471
00:40:53.407 --> 00:40:54.538
Lock-queue pileup.

472
00:40:54.818 --> 00:40:55.586
Cause:

473
00:40:55.866 --> 00:41:00.623
A slow DDL waits behind a long transaction while new queries queue behind it.

474
00:41:00.903 --> 00:41:07.389
Mitigation: locktimeout; kill long transactions before migrating; run in low-traffic window.

475
00:41:07.669 --> 00:41:08.842
Rollback breaks.

476
00:41:09.122 --> 00:41:09.890
Cause:

477
00:41:10.170 --> 00:41:13.818
Read-new deployed while old code still running, then rolled back.

478
00:41:14.098 --> 00:41:15.101
Mitigation:

479
00:41:15.381 --> 00:41:20.757
Keep dual-writing across the read-switch; drop only after a stable release (R5).

480
00:41:21.037 --> 00:41:22.125
Dead-letter flood.

481
00:41:22.405 --> 00:41:23.173
Cause:

482
00:41:23.453 --> 00:41:27.997
Message field renamed or removed; un-upgraded consumer can't parse.

483
00:41:28.277 --> 00:41:29.279
Mitigation:

484
00:41:29.559 --> 00:41:34.167
Registry FULL compatibility (L3); reserve retired field numbers.

485
00:41:34.447 --> 00:41:35.898
Backfill bloats table.

486
00:41:36.178 --> 00:41:36.946
Cause:

487
00:41:37.226 --> 00:41:41.727
One huge UPDATE, then 3M dead tuples un-vacuumable until commit.

488
00:41:42.007 --> 00:41:43.010
Mitigation:

489
00:41:43.290 --> 00:41:47.258
Batched, committed backfill (L4); watch ndeadtup, VACUUM after.

490
00:41:48.608 --> 00:41:49.952
Silent data drift.

491
00:41:50.232 --> 00:41:51.000
Cause:

492
00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:53.541
Store-to-store dual-write bug not caught.

493
00:41:53.821 --> 00:41:54.824
Mitigation:

494
00:41:55.104 --> 00:41:59.541
Continuous reconciliation before the read-switch (§2.9).

495
00:41:59.821 --> 00:42:01.613
NOT VALID never validated.

496
00:42:01.893 --> 00:42:02.661
Cause:

497
00:42:02.941 --> 00:42:05.544
Team adds NOT VALID and forgets VALIDATE.

498
00:42:05.824 --> 00:42:06.827
Mitigation:

499
00:42:07.107 --> 00:42:12.120
Constraint stays untrusted (new rows checked, old rows not); track and finish.

500
00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:15.689
Observability (Ch 24).

501
00:42:15.969 --> 00:42:24.161
Lock waits: pglocks joined to pgstatactivity shows who's blocking whom; alert on lock waits N seconds during a deploy.

502
00:42:24.441 --> 00:42:32.206
DDL duration: log every migration's wall-clock and lock-hold time; a spike means a rewrite slipped through review.

503
00:42:32.486 --> 00:42:38.374
Bloat: ndeadtup or nlivetup per table (pgstatusertables); autovacuum activity.

504
00:42:38.654 --> 00:42:45.566
Backfill progress: emit rows-done or rows-total per batch so a long backfill is observable and resumable.

505
00:42:46.916 --> 00:42:52.228
Bus: dead-letter rate per topic, and the registry's rejected-schema count in CI.

506
00:42:52.508 --> 00:42:58.524
Migration history: the schemahistory table (Flyway) is the source of truth for what's applied where.

507
00:42:59.874 --> 00:43:00.813
Security.

508
00:43:01.093 --> 00:43:10.373
Migrations often run with elevated (DDL) privileges, keep that credential separate from the app's runtime credential and out of the app image.

509
00:43:10.653 --> 00:43:19.954
A backfill that copies PII into a new column inherits the same classification, apply the same encryption or masking (Ch 23).

510
00:43:20.234 --> 00:43:27.658
Reserve retired message fields so a future reuse can't accidentally expose an old field's data to a new consumer.

511
00:43:29.008 --> 00:43:42.448
Performance and tuning knobs. locktimeout (fail fast), statementtimeout (bound runaway DDL), maintenanceworkmem (faster index builds), maxparallelmaintenanceworkers (parallel CREATE INDEX).

512
00:43:42.728 --> 00:43:48.829
Backfill batch size (throughput versus lock duration), and an inter-batch sleep under load (L4).

513
00:43:49.109 --> 00:43:59.243
Run CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY off-peak: it's slower and does two table passes, and a failed concurrent build leaves an invalid index you must drop and retry.

514
00:44:00.593 --> 00:44:01.318
Cost.

515
00:44:01.598 --> 00:44:14.163
Zero-downtime migrations cost engineering time (more steps, more than one release) and some transient storage (the new column + old column coexist; index builds need scratch space).

516
00:44:14.443 --> 00:44:19.329
The alternative, a migration outage on a checkout path, costs revenue and trust.

517
00:44:19.609 --> 00:44:23.449
The trade is almost always worth it above a few hundred thousand rows.

518
00:44:24.799 --> 00:44:25.737
Part 7.

519
00:44:26.017 --> 00:44:27.724
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

520
00:44:28.974 --> 00:44:29.891
Watch out:

521
00:44:30.171 --> 00:44:30.811
1.

522
00:44:31.091 --> 00:44:33.609
Renaming a column in place on a live table.

523
00:44:33.889 --> 00:44:34.699
ALTER...

524
00:44:34.979 --> 00:44:43.897
RENAME breaks every running instance of the old code the instant it commits, including, during a rolling deploy, pods that haven't updated yet.

525
00:44:44.177 --> 00:44:46.651
Expand or contract instead (L2).

526
00:44:48.001 --> 00:44:48.919
Watch out:

527
00:44:49.199 --> 00:44:49.903
2.

528
00:44:50.183 --> 00:44:51.249
ALTER COLUMN...

529
00:44:51.529 --> 00:44:53.940
TYPE on a big table during business hours.

530
00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:58.273
Most type changes rewrite the whole table under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE.

531
00:44:58.553 --> 00:45:02.180
Nine-minute outage on 40M rows (§1.1).

532
00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:05.788
Add a new column of the new type and expand or contract.

533
00:45:07.138 --> 00:45:08.055
Watch out:

534
00:45:08.335 --> 00:45:09.082
3.

535
00:45:09.362 --> 00:45:12.413
One unbounded UPDATE to backfill millions of rows.

536
00:45:12.693 --> 00:45:17.066
Locks every touched row until commit, blocks concurrent writers (L4:

537
00:45:17.346 --> 00:45:21.442
8 s versus 100 ms), and creates un-vacuumable bloat.

538
00:45:21.722 --> 00:45:23.173
Always batch and commit.

539
00:45:24.523 --> 00:45:25.440
Watch out:

540
00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:26.424
4.

541
00:45:26.704 --> 00:45:27.685
ADD COLUMN...

542
00:45:27.965 --> 00:45:30.099
DEFAULT volatile / DEFAULT now().

543
00:45:30.379 --> 00:45:33.749
A volatile default forces a full-table rewrite (L1:

544
00:45:34.029 --> 00:45:36.803
1.8 s under lock on 2M rows).

545
00:45:37.083 --> 00:45:40.475
A constant default is metadata-only (14 ms).

546
00:45:40.755 --> 00:45:43.763
Add nullable, or with a constant default, then backfill.

547
00:45:45.113 --> 00:45:46.030
Watch out:

548
00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:47.078
5.

549
00:45:47.358 --> 00:45:50.131
Naive SET NOT NULL on a big table.

550
00:45:50.411 --> 00:45:56.129
Scans and locks the whole table to validate (L1: stalled queries to the 8 s timeout).

551
00:45:56.409 --> 00:45:57.774
Use ADD CONSTRAINT...

552
00:45:58.054 --> 00:46:03.323
CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID then VALIDATE CONSTRAINT (which doesn't block writes).

553
00:46:04.673 --> 00:46:05.591
Watch out:

554
00:46:05.871 --> 00:46:06.617
6.

555
00:46:06.897 --> 00:46:08.881
Plain CREATE INDEX on a hot table.

556
00:46:09.161 --> 00:46:11.295
Blocks writes for the whole build (L1).

557
00:46:11.575 --> 00:46:13.388
Use CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY.

558
00:46:14.738 --> 00:46:15.655
Watch out:

559
00:46:15.935 --> 00:46:16.682
7.

560
00:46:16.962 --> 00:46:19.778
Reusing a message field number or enum value.

561
00:46:20.058 --> 00:46:26.607
Old data on the wire for that number gets misread by the new schema, silent corruption, no error (L3).

562
00:46:26.887 --> 00:46:29.554
Reserve retired numbers; never reuse.

563
00:46:30.904 --> 00:46:31.821
Watch out:

564
00:46:32.101 --> 00:46:32.784
8.

565
00:46:33.064 --> 00:46:35.453
No schema registry (or set to NONE).

566
00:46:35.733 --> 00:46:43.029
Without an enforced compatibility check, an incompatible producer ships and floods consumers' dead-letter queues in production.

567
00:46:43.309 --> 00:46:46.360
Set the registry to FULL and check in CI (L3).

568
00:46:47.710 --> 00:46:48.627
Watch out:

569
00:46:48.907 --> 00:46:49.654
9.

570
00:46:49.934 --> 00:46:51.641
Dropping the old shape too early.

571
00:46:51.921 --> 00:47:00.603
Contracting while any deployed (or rollback-target) code still reads or writes the old column or field turns a rollback into an outage.

572
00:47:00.883 --> 00:47:05.086
Keep the old shape until the new path is stable for a release (R5).

573
00:47:06.436 --> 00:47:07.353
Watch out:

574
00:47:07.633 --> 00:47:08.337
10.

575
00:47:08.617 --> 00:47:11.241
Migrating stores without reconciliation.

576
00:47:11.521 --> 00:47:16.919
Dual-write bugs are silent; you discover them after the read-switch when data is already wrong.

577
00:47:17.199 --> 00:47:22.020
Reconcile continuously and fix before switching reads (§2.9).

578
00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:24.287
Watch out:

579
00:47:24.567 --> 00:47:25.399
11.

580
00:47:25.679 --> 00:47:27.429
Down-migrations in production.

581
00:47:27.709 --> 00:47:30.781
Down-scripts are rarely tested and often destructive.

582
00:47:31.061 --> 00:47:33.621
Roll forward with a new migration instead.

583
00:47:34.971 --> 00:47:35.888
Watch out:

584
00:47:36.168 --> 00:47:36.957
12.

585
00:47:37.237 --> 00:47:39.648
NOT VALID without the follow-up VALIDATE.

586
00:47:39.928 --> 00:47:45.496
The constraint only checks new rows; old rows are never verified and the planner can't trust it.

587
00:47:45.776 --> 00:47:47.888
Always finish with VALIDATE CONSTRAINT.

588
00:47:49.238 --> 00:47:50.113
Part 8.

589
00:47:50.393 --> 00:47:51.737
Interview Q and A.

590
00:47:52.987 --> 00:47:53.819
Beginner.

591
00:47:54.099 --> 00:47:55.123
Question 1.

592
00:47:55.403 --> 00:48:00.288
What is a zero-downtime schema migration, and why can't you just run ALTER TABLE?

593
00:48:00.568 --> 00:48:01.933
Thirty-second answer:

594
00:48:02.213 --> 00:48:08.720
Zero-downtime means the schema changes while the system keeps serving traffic and while code is mid-deploy.

595
00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:25.427
You can't just ALTER because (a) many ALTERs take a strong lock and rewrite the whole table, stalling every query for the duration, and (b) during a rolling deploy old and new code run at once, so a rename or type change breaks whichever version doesn't expect it.

596
00:48:25.707 --> 00:48:29.525
You change the schema in small, backward-compatible steps instead.

597
00:48:30.875 --> 00:48:31.878
Question 2.

598
00:48:32.158 --> 00:48:34.995
Explain backward versus forward compatibility.

599
00:48:35.275 --> 00:48:36.641
Thirty-second answer:

600
00:48:36.921 --> 00:48:42.937
Backward compatible = new code can read data written by the old schema (deploy readers first).

601
00:48:43.217 --> 00:48:49.254
Forward compatible = old code can read data written by the new schema (deploy writers first).

602
00:48:49.534 --> 00:48:57.235
Full = both, which is what you need during a rolling deploy because you can't control which version reads a given row or message.

603
00:48:58.585 --> 00:48:59.631
Question 3.

604
00:48:59.911 --> 00:49:03.516
What is the expand or contract (parallel change) pattern?

605
00:49:03.796 --> 00:49:05.161
Thirty-second answer:

606
00:49:05.441 --> 00:49:17.153
To change a column safely: expand (add the new column), dual-write (write both), backfill (fill history in batches), validate, read-new (switch reads), contract (drop the old column).

607
00:49:17.433 --> 00:49:24.367
Each step is backward+forward compatible, so old and new code coexist and you can roll back at any point.

608
00:49:24.647 --> 00:49:26.460
The destructive drop is last.

609
00:49:27.810 --> 00:49:28.877
Question 4.

610
00:49:29.157 --> 00:49:32.634
Why is one big UPDATE to backfill a huge table dangerous?

611
00:49:32.914 --> 00:49:34.279
Thirty-second answer:

612
00:49:34.559 --> 00:49:50.943
It touches every row in one transaction: it locks all those rows until commit (blocking concurrent writers), writes one giant WAL record, and creates a mass of dead tuples that autovacuum can't reclaim until the transaction ends, table bloat.

613
00:49:51.223 --> 00:49:53.762
If it fails near the end, it all rolls back.

614
00:49:54.042 --> 00:49:56.858
Batch it: update key ranges and commit each batch.

615
00:49:58.208 --> 00:49:59.211
Intermediate.

616
00:49:59.491 --> 00:50:00.600
Question 5.

617
00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:05.317
Which PostgreSQL DDL operations block, and how do you avoid it?

618
00:50:05.597 --> 00:50:06.963
Thirty-second answer:

619
00:50:07.243 --> 00:50:18.933
Blocking: volatile-default ADD COLUMN, most ALTER COLUMN TYPE, naive SET NOT NULL, ADD CHECK, and plain CREATE INDEX, they take a strong lock and do a full-table operation under it.

620
00:50:19.213 --> 00:50:26.317
Safe: nullable or constant-default ADD COLUMN (metadata-only), CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY, and ADD CONSTRAINT...

621
00:50:26.597 --> 00:50:30.224
NOT VALID then VALIDATE CONSTRAINT (which doesn't block writes).

622
00:50:31.574 --> 00:50:32.683
Question 6.

623
00:50:32.963 --> 00:50:35.395
How do you rename a column with no downtime?

624
00:50:35.675 --> 00:50:37.041
Thirty-second answer:

625
00:50:37.321 --> 00:50:39.753
You don't rename, you expand or contract.

626
00:50:40.033 --> 00:50:49.995
Add the new column, dual-write both, backfill history in batches, switch reads to the new column (still dual-writing), then drop the old column once nothing uses it.

627
00:50:50.275 --> 00:50:54.990
A rename in place would break every not-yet-upgraded pod the instant it runs.

628
00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:57.428
Question 7.

629
00:50:57.708 --> 00:51:01.143
How do Protobuf and Avro each handle schema evolution?

630
00:51:01.423 --> 00:51:02.788
Thirty-second answer:

631
00:51:03.068 --> 00:51:18.236
Protobuf keys fields by number: old readers skip unknown numbers (forward-compat) and new readers default missing ones (backward-compat), so adding fields and even renaming is safe, but reusing a number or changing a type is not.

632
00:51:18.516 --> 00:51:27.092
Avro uses both a writer and reader schema and resolves between them, so adding or removing fields needs defaults to stay compatible.

633
00:51:27.372 --> 00:51:29.697
Both are enforced by a schema registry.

634
00:51:31.047 --> 00:51:32.071
Question 8.

635
00:51:32.351 --> 00:51:34.677
What are schema-registry compatibility modes?

636
00:51:34.957 --> 00:51:36.322
Thirty-second answer:

637
00:51:36.602 --> 00:51:46.415
BACKWARD (new schema reads old data, deploy consumers first), FORWARD (old schema reads new data, deploy producers first), FULL (both), and NONE (no checks).

638
00:51:46.695 --> 00:51:54.141
The registry rejects a schema that violates the configured mode at publish time, so an incompatible producer never ships.

639
00:51:55.491 --> 00:51:56.557
Question 9.

640
00:51:56.837 --> 00:52:00.955
How do you version an HTTP API without breaking clients?

641
00:52:01.235 --> 00:52:02.600
Thirty-second answer:

642
00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:08.384
Make additive changes (new optional fields) in place, clients ignore unknown fields.

643
00:52:08.664 --> 00:52:25.901
Only introduce a new version (/v2) for true breaks (removed or renamed fields, changed types or semantics), run it alongside or v1, migrate clients, and retire or v1 with Deprecation or Sunset headers after monitoring who still calls it.

644
00:52:27.251 --> 00:52:28.019
Senior.

645
00:52:28.299 --> 00:52:29.345
Question 10.

646
00:52:29.625 --> 00:52:36.878
Walk through migrating orders.total (bigint paise) to amount (numeric rupees) on a 40M-row live table.

647
00:52:37.158 --> 00:52:38.523
Thirty-second answer:

648
00:52:38.803 --> 00:52:40.233
Expand or contract.

649
00:52:40.513 --> 00:52:45.035
(1) ADD COLUMN amount numeric(12,2) nullable, metadata-only.

650
00:52:45.315 --> 00:52:48.643
(2) Deploy dual-write: writes set both total and amount.

651
00:52:48.923 --> 00:52:56.091
(3) Batched backfill amount = total or 100.0 over PK ranges, committing each batch.

652
00:52:56.371 --> 00:53:01.961
(4) ADD CONSTRAINT amountpresent CHECK (amount IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID then VALIDATE.

653
00:53:02.241 --> 00:53:05.441
(5) Deploy read-new (reads amount, still dual-writes).

654
00:53:05.721 --> 00:53:08.793
(6) After a stable release, DROP COLUMN total.

655
00:53:09.073 --> 00:53:11.078
Never rename or retype in place.

656
00:53:12.428 --> 00:53:13.559
Question 11.

657
00:53:13.839 --> 00:53:18.383
How do you migrate a dataset from one datastore to another with no downtime?

658
00:53:18.663 --> 00:53:20.028
Thirty-second answer:

659
00:53:20.308 --> 00:53:39.999
Dual-write to old and new (ideally via an outbox or CDC so you avoid a distributed transaction), backfill history in batches, continuously reconcile old versus new (counts, checksums, sampled diffs) and fix the dual-write bugs it finds, switch reads to the new store behind a flag with a canary,

660
00:53:40.279 --> 00:53:44.673
keep the old store as fallback, and decommission only after reads are stable.

661
00:53:46.023 --> 00:53:47.111
Question 12.

662
00:53:47.391 --> 00:53:51.189
Why set locktimeout on migrations, and what's a lock-queue pileup?

663
00:53:51.469 --> 00:53:52.834
Thirty-second answer:

664
00:53:53.114 --> 00:54:07.386
If a migration's ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock request can't be granted immediately (a long transaction holds a conflicting lock), it waits at the head of the lock queue, and every new query that needs the table then queues behind the migration.

665
00:54:07.666 --> 00:54:18.418
So one slow migration behind one slow transaction can stall the whole table. locktimeout makes the migration give up quickly and retry, instead of triggering that pileup.

666
00:54:19.768 --> 00:54:21.048
Question 13.

667
00:54:21.328 --> 00:54:25.232
What exactly happens on the wire when you reuse a Protobuf field number?

668
00:54:25.512 --> 00:54:26.877
Thirty-second answer:

669
00:54:27.157 --> 00:54:31.360
Protobuf identifies fields by number + wire type, not name.

670
00:54:31.640 --> 00:54:45.208
If you reuse number 3 (previously an int64) as a string, old data on the wire carries a varint for 3, but the new schema expects a length-delimited value, so the new reader can't map it to the string field.

671
00:54:45.488 --> 00:54:50.587
It ends up as an unknown field and the declared string reads as its default (empty).

672
00:54:50.867 --> 00:54:53.064
No exception, silent data loss.

673
00:54:54.414 --> 00:54:55.609
Question 14.

674
00:54:55.889 --> 00:54:58.982
Compare NOT NULL via full validation versus CHECK...

675
00:54:59.262 --> 00:55:00.947
NOT VALID + VALIDATE.

676
00:55:01.227 --> 00:55:02.593
Thirty-second answer:

677
00:55:02.873 --> 00:55:04.430
A naive ALTER COLUMN...

678
00:55:04.710 --> 00:55:12.475
SET NOT NULL scans and locks the whole table under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE to prove no nulls exist, blocking everyone.

679
00:55:12.755 --> 00:55:29.203
Adding a CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID is a fast metadata-only step (new rows are checked, existing rows aren't), and a subsequent VALIDATE CONSTRAINT scans the table under a weaker SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE lock that does not block reads or writes.

680
00:55:29.483 --> 00:55:31.382
Same guarantee, no outage.

681
00:55:32.732 --> 00:55:33.628
Architect.

682
00:55:33.908 --> 00:55:35.103
Question 15.

683
00:55:35.383 --> 00:55:41.740
Design an org-wide standard so schema or message or API changes never cause an outage.

684
00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:43.385
Thirty-second answer:

685
00:55:43.665 --> 00:55:44.583
Codify:

686
00:55:44.863 --> 00:56:02.569
(1) online-DDL rules with enforced locktimeout and a CI linter that rejects rewriting DDL; (2) expand or contract for all column changes; (3) batched backfills only; (4) Protobuf or Avro on the bus under a registry set to FULL,

687
00:56:02.849 --> 00:56:19.425
checked in CI; (5) additive-first APIs with or vN only for breaks and Sunset headers; (6) forward-only versioned migrations run as a pipeline step before deploy; (7) store migrations via dual-write + reconcile.

688
00:56:19.705 --> 00:56:22.308
Gate destructive steps on usage telemetry.

689
00:56:23.658 --> 00:56:24.853
Question 16.

690
00:56:25.133 --> 00:56:28.631
How do blue-green deployment and schema migration interact?

691
00:56:28.911 --> 00:56:30.277
Thirty-second answer:

692
00:56:30.557 --> 00:56:36.167
Blue-green swaps whole app environments, but blue and green almost always share one database.

693
00:56:36.447 --> 00:56:42.911
So the schema must be compatible with both colors simultaneously, which is exactly expand or contract.

694
00:56:43.191 --> 00:56:54.711
You run the additive (expand) migration before cutting traffic to green, keep dual-writing so you can cut back to blue, and only contract after green is confirmed and blue is retired.

695
00:56:56.061 --> 00:56:57.405
Question 17.

696
00:56:57.685 --> 00:57:00.971
A downstream team can't upgrade their consumer for months.

697
00:57:01.251 --> 00:57:02.765
How do you evolve the event?

698
00:57:03.045 --> 00:57:04.411
Thirty-second answer:

699
00:57:04.691 --> 00:57:14.483
Keep the event FULL-compatible: only add fields, never remove or rename or retype or reuse numbers, so their old consumer keeps working untouched.

700
00:57:14.763 --> 00:57:29.845
If you genuinely need a breaking change, publish a new event type or topic version alongside the old, dual-produce to both, and let them migrate on their timeline before you retire the old one, same expand or contract shape, applied to the topic.

701
00:57:31.195 --> 00:57:32.369
Question 18.

702
00:57:32.649 --> 00:57:36.894
How do you make a huge backfill resumable, throttled, and safe under load?

703
00:57:37.174 --> 00:57:38.539
Thirty-second answer:

704
00:57:38.819 --> 00:57:45.049
Drive it off an indexed key range and persist the last-completed key so a crash resumes where it stopped.

705
00:57:45.329 --> 00:57:46.438
Commit each batch.

706
00:57:46.718 --> 00:57:53.651
Throttle with an inter-batch sleep and or or by watching replication lag and lock waits, backing off when they rise.

707
00:57:53.931 --> 00:57:58.411
Make it idempotent (WHERE newcol IS NULL) so re-running a batch is harmless.

708
00:57:58.691 --> 00:58:00.142
Emit progress metrics.

709
00:58:01.492 --> 00:58:02.729
Question 19.

710
00:58:03.009 --> 00:58:06.636
What's your rollback strategy at each phase of a schema migration?

711
00:58:06.916 --> 00:58:08.281
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:58:08.561 --> 00:58:14.663
During expand or dual-write: roll back the code freely, the old column still works and is still written.

713
00:58:14.943 --> 00:58:18.356
During backfill: it's idempotent and additive; just stop it.

714
00:58:18.636 --> 00:58:25.100
During read-new: roll back the code, because you kept dual-writing, the old column is still current and readable.

715
00:58:25.380 --> 00:58:30.991
Only contract (drop) is irreversible, which is why it's last and gated on a stable release.

716
00:58:31.271 --> 00:58:35.495
Rollbacks are always forward-only new migrations, never down-scripts.

717
00:58:36.845 --> 00:58:37.954
Question 20.

718
00:58:38.234 --> 00:58:42.522
When is a maintenance window (accepting downtime) actually the right call?

719
00:58:42.802 --> 00:58:44.167
Thirty-second answer:

720
00:58:44.447 --> 00:59:07.103
When the change genuinely has no compatible incremental path and the online approach would cost more than a short, scheduled outage, for example, a fundamental primary-key type change on a modest table, a storage-engine migration, or a small internal tool where the engineering cost of expand or contract dwarfs the impact of five minutes down at 3 a.m.

721
00:59:07.383 --> 00:59:14.039
You make it a conscious decision with stakeholder sign-off, a tested runbook, and a rehearsed rollback, not an accident.

722
00:59:15.389 --> 00:59:16.285
Part 9.

723
00:59:16.565 --> 00:59:17.973
Summary and Practice.

724
00:59:19.223 --> 00:59:20.311
Key takeaways.

725
00:59:20.591 --> 00:59:24.517
Every intermediate state must be compatible with old and new code.

726
00:59:24.797 --> 00:59:27.719
Rolling deploys and rollbacks run both at once.

727
00:59:27.999 --> 00:59:30.346
This single rule generates everything else.

728
00:59:30.626 --> 00:59:31.714
Know what locks.

729
00:59:31.994 --> 00:59:44.090
Metadata-only adds and CONCURRENTLY or VALIDATE are safe; volatile-default adds, in-place ALTER TYPE, naive SET NOT NULL and plain CREATE INDEX stall live traffic (L1).

730
00:59:44.370 --> 00:59:52.349
Change columns by expand or contract: add, then dual-write, then batched backfill, then validate, then read-new, then drop.

731
00:59:52.629 --> 00:59:54.869
The destructive step is last (L2).

732
00:59:56.219 --> 01:00:02.320
Backfill in committed batches, off an indexed key range, never one unbounded UPDATE (L4).

733
01:00:02.600 --> 01:00:08.211
Message and API schemas are append-only contracts with parties you can't redeploy.

734
01:00:08.491 --> 01:00:14.059
Evolve them under registry compatibility modes; reserve retired field numbers (L3).

735
01:00:14.339 --> 01:00:22.403
Migrate stores with dual-write + backfill + reconcile + flagged read-switch; reconciliation catches the silent bugs.

736
01:00:22.683 --> 01:00:28.251
Set locktimeout so a blocked migration fails fast instead of causing a lock-queue pileup.

737
01:00:29.601 --> 01:00:34.443
Roll forward, not down, and gate the irreversible drop on usage telemetry.

738
01:00:35.793 --> 01:00:37.116
Decision cheat sheet.

739
01:00:37.396 --> 01:00:38.740
Here is a quick table:

740
01:00:39.020 --> 01:00:40.641
Situation, and Do this.

741
01:00:40.921 --> 01:00:41.924
Add a column:

742
01:00:42.204 --> 01:00:46.940
Nullable or constant default (metadata-only); backfill in batches if needed.

743
01:00:47.220 --> 01:00:48.884
Rename or retype a column:

744
01:00:49.164 --> 01:00:51.532
Expand or contract (never in place).

745
01:00:51.812 --> 01:00:53.476
Add an index on a hot table:

746
01:00:53.756 --> 01:00:55.313
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY.

747
01:00:55.593 --> 01:00:57.087
Add NOT NULL or a CHECK:

748
01:00:57.367 --> 01:00:58.519
ADD CONSTRAINT...

749
01:00:58.799 --> 01:01:00.420
NOT VALID then VALIDATE.

750
01:01:00.700 --> 01:01:02.215
Backfill millions of rows:

751
01:01:02.495 --> 01:01:05.183
Batched, committed, key-range, idempotent, throttled.

752
01:01:05.463 --> 01:01:07.063
Change an event or message:

753
01:01:07.343 --> 01:01:13.465
Additive only; registry FULL; reserve retired numbers; new topic version for real breaks.

754
01:01:13.745 --> 01:01:15.004
Change an API:

755
01:01:15.284 --> 01:01:19.636
Additive in place; /vN + Sunset headers only for breaks.

756
01:01:20.986 --> 01:01:22.586
Move data between stores:

757
01:01:22.866 --> 01:01:27.922
Dual-write (outbox), then backfill, then reconcile, then read-switch, then decommission.

758
01:01:28.202 --> 01:01:30.719
Truly no compatible path, low impact:

759
01:01:30.999 --> 01:01:33.218
A conscious, tested maintenance window.

760
